"[1512.08031] Orthogonal and unitary tensor decomposition from an algebraic perspective (https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08031)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.08031\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Algebraic Geometry\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 25 Dec 2015]\nL9: # Title:Orthogonal and unitary tensor decomposition from an algebraic perspective\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Ada Boralevi , cite6†Jan Draisma , cite7†Emil Horobet , cite8†Elina Robeva L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Orthogonal and unitary tensor decomposition from an algebraic perspective, by Ada Boralevi and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:While every matrix admits a singular value decomposition, in which the terms are pairwise orthogonal in a strong sense, higher-order tensors typically do not admit such an orthogonal decomposition. Those that do have attracted attention from theoretical computer science and scientific computing. We complement this existing body of literature with an algebro-geometric analysis of the set of orthogonally decomposable tensors.\nL17: > More specifically, we prove that they form a real-algebraic variety defined by polynomials of degree at most four. The exact degrees, and the corresponding polynomials, are different in each of three times two scenarios: ordinary, symmetric, or alternating tensors; and real-orthogonal versus complex-unitary.\nL18: A key feature of our approach is a surprising connection between orthogonally decomposable tensors and semisimple algebras---associative in the ordinary and symmetric settings and of compact Lie type in the alternating setting.\nL19: Subjects:  | Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:1512.08031 [math.AG]\nL21:    | (or cite12†arXiv:1512.08031v1 [math.AG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1512.08031†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Elina Robeva [cite14†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 25 Dec 2015 20:31:41 UTC (78 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Orthogonal and unitary tensor decomposition from an algebraic perspective, by Ada Boralevi and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite9†View PDF L35:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite15†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite16†view license L39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: math.AG\nL43: \nL44: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L45: \nL46: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2015-12 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite22†math L51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: ### cite26†2 blog links L58: \nL59: (cite27†what is this?†info.arxiv.org )\nL60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Connected Papers (cite29†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL83: \nL84: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL85: \nL86: Litmaps (cite30†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL87: