org · cite53†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L165: \nL166: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1705.10404] Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10404)\nciteturn0view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10404\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Numerical Analysis\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 May 2017]\nL9: # Title:Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Cun Mu , cite6†Daniel Hsu , cite7†Donald Goldfarb L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors, by Cun Mu and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Many idealized problems in signal processing, machine learning and statistics can be reduced to the problem of finding the symmetric canonical decomposition of an underlying symmetric and orthogonally decomposable (SOD) tensor.\nL17: Drawing inspiration from the matrix case, the successive rank-one approximations (SROA) scheme has been proposed and shown to yield this tensor decomposition exactly, and a plethora of numerical methods have thus been developed for the tensor rank-one approximation problem.\nL18: In practice, however, the inevitable errors (say) from estimation, computation, and modeling necessitate that the input tensor can only be assumed to be a nearly SOD tensor---i.e., a symmetric tensor slightly perturbed from the underlying SOD tensor. This article shows that even in the presence of perturbation, SROA can still robustly recover the symmetric canonical decomposition of the underlying tensor.\nL19: It is shown that when the perturbation error is small enough, the approximation errors do not accumulate with the iteration number. Numerical results are presented to support the theoretical findings.\nL20: Subjects:  | Numerical Analysis (math.NA)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:1705.10404 [math.NA]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:1705.10404v1 [math.NA] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1705.10404†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications 36.4 (2015): 1638-1659\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite13†https://doi.org/10.1137/15M1010890†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Cun Mu [cite14†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Mon, 29 May 2017 21:37:32 UTC (2,162 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors, by Cun Mu and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite8†View PDF L38:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite15†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite16†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: math.NA\nL45: \nL46: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L47: \nL48: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2017-05 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite22†cs L53: cite23†cs.NA L54: cite24†math L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite27†Semantic Sc