162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Spectral Theory\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 24 Aug 2010 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 1 Nov 2010 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:A Hilbert-Schmidt analog of Huaxin Lin's Theorem\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Nikolay Filonov , cite7†Ilya Kachkovskiy L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Hilbert-Schmidt analog of Huaxin Lin's Theorem, by Nikolay Filonov and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The paper is devoted to the following question: consider two self-adjoint $n\\times n$-matrices $H_1,H_2$, $\\|H_1\\|\\le 1$, $\\|H_2\\|\\le 1$, such that their commutator $[H_1,H_2]$ is small in some sence. Do there exist such self-adjoint commuting matrices $A_1,A_2$, such that $A_i$ is close to $H_i$, $i=1,2$? The answer to this question is positive if the smallness is considered with respect to the operator norm.\nL17: The following result was established by Huaxin Lin: if $\\|[H_1,H_2]\\|=\\delta$, then we can choose $A_i$ such that $\\|H_i-A_i\\|\\le C(\\delta)$, $i=1,2$, where $C(\\delta)\\to 0$ as $\\delta\\to 0$. Notice that $C(\\delta)$ does not depend on $n$. The proof was simplified by Friis and Rørdam. A quantitative version of the result with $C(\\delta)=E(1/\\delta)\\delta^{1/5}$, where $E(x)$ grows slower than any power of $x$, was recently established by Hastings.\nL18: We are interested in the same question, but with respect to the normalized Hilbert-Schmidt norm. An analog of Lin's theorem for this norm was established by Hadwin and independently by Filonov and Safarov. A quantitative version with $C(\\delta)=12\\delta^{1/6}$, where $\\delta=\\|[H_1,H_2]\\|_{\\tr}$, was recently obtained by Glebsky. In the present paper, we use the same ideas to prove a similar result with $C(\\delta)=2\\delta^{1/4}$. We also refine Glebsky's theorem concerning the case of $n$ operators.\nL19: Comments:  | LaTeX, 5 pages. Important references added, and the case of $n$ operators is considered\nL20: Subjects:  | Spectral Theory (math.SP); Operator Algebras (math.OA)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:1008.4002 [math.SP]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:1008.4002v2 [math.SP] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1008.4002†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Ilya Kachkovskiy [cite13†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Tue, 24 Aug 2010 10:09:45 UTC (3 KB)\nL28: [v2] Mon, 1 Nov 2010 11:28:54 UTC (5 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled A Hilbert-Schmidt analog of Huaxin Lin's Theorem, by Nikolay Filonov and 1 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: math.SP\nL44: \nL45: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L46: \nL47: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2010-08 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite21†math L52: cite22†math.OA L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \n"
